// CARD a1-01 · SEE: a real map of Billund zooming in; on "Danish" a red pin drops on the town; on "Billund" a circle opens from the pin into Ole Kirk's real red-brick house · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@map_dive;map:lego_map_billund;x:.48;y:.69;go:Danish;iris:Billund;photo:lego_ole_kirks_house_01;sub:OLE KIRK'S HOUSE · BILLUND;in:right"
It started with wood, in a carpenter's workshop in the Danish village of **Billund**.

// CARD a1-02 · SEE: a carpenter's hands planing a board; on "Ole" his real portrait slides in as an old print and settles · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@print_slide;clip:lego_clip_planing_shavings;pic:lego_ole_kirk_portrait;go:Ole;side:left;cap:OLE KIRK CHRISTIANSEN;sub:carpenter · from 1932;out:right"
In nineteen thirty-two, Ole Kirk Christiansen began making wooden **toys**.

// CARD a1-03 · SEE: the real 1936 logo dim; LEG and GODT slide in; on "well" the unused letters fall out and LEGO closes up in gold · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@word_join;bg:lego_logo_1936;a:LEG;b:GODT;keep:0,1,4,5;go:well;gloss:leg godt · play well;in:right"
He took the name from two Danish words, leg godt: play **well**. (J)

// CARD a1-04 · SEE: a 1930s workshop (AI moment art, no people); a plank crosses the frame and on "motto" the motto is carved into it letter by letter, chips flying · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@carve_line;bg:lego_scene_workshop_1930s;line:ONLY THE BEST IS GOOD ENOUGH;go:motto;span:1.3;sub:the motto, 1936;out:right"
His motto, from nineteen thirty-six: only the best is good **enough**.

// CARD a1-05 · SEE: the real wooden LEGO trains; a ruler slides in with 1932…1960 on its ticks; on "first" a red line runs its whole length; 28 YEARS OF WOOD · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@year_ruler;bg:lego_wooden_trains_history;y0:1932;y1:1960;go:first;span:1.4;lab:28 YEARS OF WOOD;in:right"
For its first twenty-eight years, LEGO was a wood **company**.

// CARD a1-06 · SEE: plastic granules pouring; on "early" a bright edge sweeps across and reveals LEGO's real early plastic toys · SEAM out right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@wipe_to_photo;clip:lego_clip_plastic_granules;pic:lego_early_plastic_tractors;go:early;lab:EARLY LEGO PLASTIC · 1950s;sub:the first bricks followed Kiddicraft's design, from Britain;out:right"
Plastic came after the war, and LEGO's early bricks followed a British design, Kiddicraft's self-locking **brick**.

// CARD a1-07 · SEE: LEGOLAND's real Miniland, a whole brick world; on "added" gold threads join its shop, bus, house and car into one loop; ONE SYSTEM · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@system_links;clip:lego_legoland_overview;pts:.18,.42~.4,.62~.62,.4~.82,.6~.55,.75;go:added;lab:ONE SYSTEM;in:right"
{warm} What LEGO added wasn't the brick. It was a **system**.

// CARD a1-08 · SEE: LEGOLAND's real 1970s Miniland town; on "Godtfred" his real portrait slides in from the right as a print · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@print_slide;clip:lego_legoland_1977;pic:lego_godtfred_cropped;go:Godtfred;side:right;cap:GODTFRED KIRK CHRISTIANSEN;sub:the System of Play · 1955;out:right"
In nineteen fifty-five, Ole's son Godtfred launched the System of **Play**.

// CARD a1-09 · SEE: the real 1957 photo of a boy building; on "owned" two outline bricks lift off different parts of his bricks and lock together · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@interlock_overlay;pic:lego_brick_1957_early;a:.62,.62;b:.3,.78;go:every;lab:WORKS WITH WHAT YOU OWN;in:right"
So every new set would work with every set a child already **owned**.

// CARD a1-10 · SEE: the camera rides down the real 1958 patent page for the brick, drawing by drawing, toward the fix · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@reel;under:lego_patent_brick;ua:0;move:down;z:.3;out:right"
But the bricks didn't grip yet. The fix came in nineteen fifty-**eight**.

// CARD a1-11 · SEE: a big red brick under the lamp; on "patent" a scan bar sweeps across and turns it into the patent drawing of its inside, tubes showing · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@patent_xray;doc:lego_doc_patent_us3005282_drawing_sheet1;crop:.56,.335,.8,.43;go:patent;lab:TUBES INSIDE;sub:patent filed 28 January 1958;in:right"
On the twenty-eighth of January, LEGO filed a patent: tubes inside the brick, so it **clutches**.

// CARD a1-12 · SEE: Ole Kirk's real portrait; on "Ole" its colour drains and a black border draws round it; 11 MARCH 1958 types beside his name · SEAM hard cut · HOLD
# cine act=fall fx="code:@mourning_frame;pic:lego_ole_kirk_1957;go:Ole;name:OLE KIRK CHRISTIANSEN;date:11 MARCH 1958;fx:.6;fy:.4"
Ole Kirk died a few weeks **later**.

// CARD a1-13 · SEE: the real early wooden toys; on "fire" real flames rise from the bottom through the picture, charring it; FEBRUARY 1960 · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@fire_through_photo;pic:lego_wooden_toys_history;fire:stock_nature_fire_embers_01;go:fire;span:2.2;lab:FEBRUARY 1960;in:right"
In February nineteen sixty, a fire hit the wooden-toy **workshop**.

// CARD a1-14 · SEE: two prints, wooden trains left, plastic tractors right; on "stopped" the wood greys and slides away and the plastic takes the centre · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@shelf_switch;a:lego_wooden_trains_history;b:lego_early_plastic_tractors;alab:WOOD · STOPPED;blab:PLASTIC ONLY;go:stopped;out:right"
{grave} Godtfred stopped making wooden toys, and his brothers **left**.

// CARD a1-15 · SEE: a moulding press working; on "bricks" four bricks pop out one per colour and land in a short row; FOUR COLOURS · FEW SHAPES · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@press_out;clip:lego_clip_injection_machine;go:bricks;n:4;lab:FOUR COLOURS · FEW SHAPES;in:right"
From then on LEGO made one thing: plastic bricks, in limited shapes and limited **colours**.

// CARD a1-16 · MOTIF · SEE: a real 2x4 brick close-up, studs marked LEGO; the red brick drops and lands beside it; THE LIMIT sets above · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_brick_2x4_stud;go:limit;x:.72;y:.68;h:.12;lab:THE LIMIT;lx:.5;z:.25;move:right;out:right"
Remember that limit. It's what made the system **work**.
